# Line endings are load-bearing in this repository, not cosmetic.
#
# The gate is a bash script, and it decides every phase document with
# `grep -qx '## Information items'`, which matches a whole line. A contributor
# on Windows with core.autocrlf=true — the Git for Windows default — checks
# these files out with CRLF, that grep then fails on all fourteen phases at
# once, and the gate reports fourteen false violations instead of one real
# problem. A gate that cannot tell a line-ending problem from a content problem
# is worse than no gate.
#
# core.autocrlf is a local setting. This repository cannot ship it, so this file
# is the only thing that reaches the contributor's checkout.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Bash reads a CRLF shebang as "bash\r" and the kernel answers "bad
# interpreter", so these are pinned unconditionally rather than left to the
# default above.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# PowerShell accepts either ending, and CRLF is what its own tooling writes.
# This is a preference, not a correctness rule, and it is listed so that a
# change to the default above cannot silently reclassify these.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.psm1  text eol=crlf
*.psd1  text eol=crlf

# The Windows command processor is the one case that genuinely needs CRLF.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Named explicitly because the gate's decisions depend on them, so they must not
# be left to whatever "auto" decides.
*.md          text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
LICENSE       text eol=lf
LICENSE-CODE  text eol=lf
VERSION       text eol=lf
